// assets/lore.cpp — the old books found in places (game/structures.js LORE, bookPages): title and pages by key.
// The text table is the original's, dumped with tools/port/jsdump.py ("LORE").
#include "assets/lore.h"
#include "common/fs.h"
#include <map>

namespace dq {

static const char* LORE_JSON = R"DQJSON({"first_fire":["The First Fire","Before the sky had islands, the world burned from beneath. The old miners say the Ember Depths are what is left of that fire: a sea of lava under a roof of red stone, and in its rock, cinderite, the metal that will not melt.\n\nThey say too that the floor of our world is only a crust over that fire. Strike it with a pick of mithril and it gives way, and down you go."],"sky_islands":["Of Islands in the Sky","Above the clouds the land floats in pieces, and the winds there are kind. Step off the edge and they will carry you down through the cloud to the world below, slowly, so that you land on your feet.\n\nGoing up is harder. Build high enough, or fly, and the same winds catch you at the top of the sky and set you on the nearest island. The sheep there have wings. Do not ask me how."],"watcher":["A Mage's Warning","I have seen him three times now, at the edge of the trees, always at the edge. He does not walk. He is simply there, and then not.\n\nThe elders say that where the first village is found, a door opens, a rift into a place that is his. Four keys of time close it, and the keepers of the deep places hold them. I do not know who wrote that first. I know that the torches I find in the tunnels are not mine."],"six_wyrms":["The Seven Wyrms","Each wild place has its dragon. The green one sleeps in the deep forest, the gold one in the sand, the white on the cold mountains, the brown in the bog, the red in the Depths, the blue on the high islands, and the sea-green on the floor of the open sea.\n\nTheir eggs are cold until they are warmed, by fire for most, by the sea for the last. Raise one from the shell and it will carry you anywhere, even into the sky."],"labyrinth":["Labyrinth Journal","Day 3. The stair went down and down, and then the walls began. They all look the same. I keep my right hand on the wall as my father taught me, and I will come out again, he said, wherever I go in.\n\nDay 4. There is something in the middle. I heard it breathe. It has horns. It ran at me and struck the wall where I had been standing, and the whole maze shook, and it stood there dazed. I did not wait to see it wake."],"schools":["On the Schools of Magic","Destruction is the first school anyone learns, and the least. Fire and frost come easily to the angry.\n\nRestoration is harder, for mending wants patience. Alteration changes what is: a light that follows, the air made to hold you, skin made stone. Conjuration calls what is not there, and it is the most dangerous, for what you call may not wish to leave.\n\nRead the tomes slowly. A tome read too early burns the mind and teaches nothing."],"drowned_temple":["The Drowned Temple","The fishermen of the coast speak of halls under the deep water, built of a green stone that glows, older than any kingdom. At their heart, they say, lies a block of gold the size of a man, and the drowned walk the halls to guard it.\n\nTake a sponge if you go, the old women say, and do not let your breath run out on the stair."],"miner":["A Miner's Last Entry","The new seam is good. Iron and gold and redstone in the walls, and the timbers hold. Jory says he hears skittering at night past the fourth crossing.\n\nThe webs are thicker every morning. There is a cage down there, iron, old, and spiders come out of it as though it breathes them. We have stopped going past the fourth crossing. I am leaving the chest where it is."],"desert":["The Sands Keep Secrets","Under the blue stone at the heart of the desert temples lies the treasure of the old kings, four chests of it. Many have gone down for it. Few came up.\n\nIf you would be one of the few: do not drop down the shaft. Dig the stairs beside it. And whatever you do, do not step on the stone plate in the middle of the floor below."],"villages":["On the Villages","The folk of the villages keep their own hours: to work at dawn, to the square in the afternoon, home to bed at night. They trade for emeralds and remember who has helped them.\n\nIf you strike one by mistake, say you are sorry. The guards will listen once. They will not listen twice."],"keepers":["The Keepers Below","Under the land lie the old crypts, nests and hollows, and in the deepest room of each a keeper waits: the Grave Warden, the Rot King, the Broodmother, the Hex Matron.\n\nEach calls its servants when it weakens. Each, when it falls, leaves behind a fragment of something it should never have held: a piece of time itself."],"sailor":["A Sailor's Log","Storm three days. Lost the mainmast. The captain says we are near the coast; I say we are near the bottom.\n\nIf anyone finds this: the stores are in the bow, under the deck. The captain keeps the pay chest in his cabin at the stern, and he keeps it locked, but the sea has a key for everything."],"archmage":["The Hollow Archmage","He was the greatest of us. He built the tower with his own hands and filled it with books, and his apprentices came from every village to learn.\n\nThen he found the rift. He studied it for a year. He came back from it hollow: the same robes, the same voice, nothing behind the eyes. His apprentices stayed, and serve him still. He waits at the top of the tower, among his crystals. Do not go up alone."],"crust":["Of the Stacked Worlds","The world is built in floors, like a house. The Skylands are the attic, the Ember Depths the cellar, and we live in between.\n\nThe floors are joined. A fall from the attic ends in the house; a hole through the floor of the house ends in the cellar. Only the stairs between are hidden: in cloud above, in fire below.\n\nAnd the cellar has a floor of its own. Under the last of the fire there is a crust that hums, and under that, nothing at all: a black sky, and far below it, pale islands."],"end":["The End of All Roads","There is a last place, beyond all the others, where the land is pale and the sky is black and the tall ones walk who will not be looked at.\n\nThe way there is a door of twelve eyes, deep in a stronghold under the land. Find the eyes. They will show you the way.\n\nOr go down. Always down, past the fire, until there is no more down."],"rhyme":["A Children's Rhyme","Torches two by the old pine tree,\nwho put them there? Not you, not me.\nDoors that open, signs that speak,\nhe watches us from week to week.\n\nDon't look long and don't look near,\nand if he stares, don't show your fear.\nFour keys of time to close the door,\nand then he won't come any more."],"deepdark":["The Silent City","We built it deep, where nothing could hear us. Then we learned that something could.\n\nWalk softly there. Do not run. When the crust begins to scream, it is telling you how close it is, and the fourth time it screams, it is no longer telling you anything.\n\nIt cannot see. It does not need to."],"skylands_temple":["The Queen of the Air","Before the islands broke from the world, a people lived on them who could ride the wind. When they were gone, one stayed: the Queen, in her temple above the clouds, keeping a hall for a court that will not come back.\n\nShe does not take kindly to visitors. She carries a lance that the wind itself obeys, and a piece of something older than all of us."],"ember_lord":["The Ember Lord","The keeps in the lava sea were built to hold the fire back. They did not. The fire came up through the floors and into their lord, and he burns still, in his hall, wearing a crown that never cools.\n\nThe smiths say his crown keeps the fire off whoever wears it. The smiths say a great many things."],"deep_halls":["The Keeper's Mural","Those who kept these halls trusted no lock they could not change. The way into the treasury, they set in the levers of the hall beside it, and painted the setting on the wall of the keeper's room, green for pulled and red for left alone.\n\nThe keeper is long dead. The paint is not."],"twin_vault":["The Twin Wardens","Two stood guard over the vault, the Sun and the Moon, and each one's death was only the other's grief: fell one, and the other calls it back.\n\nOnly a pair could open the door, and only a pair could hope to pass them."]})DQJSON";

const LoreBook* loreBook(const std::string& key) {
  static std::map<std::string, LoreBook> books;
  static bool loaded = false;
  if (!loaded) {
    loaded = true;
    json j = json::parse(LORE_JSON, nullptr, false);
    if (j.is_object()) for (auto& [k, v] : j.items()) {
      if (!v.is_array() || v.size() < 2) continue;
      LoreBook b; b.title = v[0].get<std::string>();
      // a book's text cut into pages of a comfortable length (bookPages)
      std::string text = v[1].get<std::string>(), cur;
      size_t at = 0;
      while (true) {
        size_t n = text.find("\n\n", at);
        std::string para = text.substr(at, n == std::string::npos ? n : n - at);
        if (cur.size() + para.size() > 520 && !cur.empty()) { b.pages.push_back(cur); cur.clear(); }
        cur += (cur.empty() ? "" : "\n\n") + para;
        if (n == std::string::npos) break;
        at = n + 2;
      }
      if (!cur.empty()) b.pages.push_back(cur);
      books[k] = std::move(b);
    }
  }
  auto it = books.find(key);
  return it == books.end() ? nullptr : &it->second;
}

} // namespace dq
